Assume condition (2). Fix $a\in X$ and $\varepsilon>0$. The <superlevel set>
$$
F=\{x:f(x)\geq f(a)+\varepsilon\}
$$
is a <closed set> and does not contain $a$. Its <complement> is therefore an <open> <neighbourhood> of $a$, so some <open ball> $B(a,r)$ avoids $F$. Consequently $f(x)<f(a)+\varepsilon$ throughout that ball, giving
$$
M_a(r)\leq f(a)+\varepsilon.
$$
The same bound holds for every smaller radius. Taking $r\downarrow0$ and then $\varepsilon\downarrow0$ yields
$$
\boxed{\lim_{r\downarrow0}\sup_{x\in B(a,r)}f(x)\leq f(a).}
$$
Together with the previous implication this proves the equivalence. A <supremum> may equal $f(a)+\varepsilon$ even when every individual value is strictly smaller; the non-strict estimate here handles that possibility.

