The <hyperbolic sine> satisfies $\sinh z=(e^z-e^{-z})/2$. The <complex exponential function> is <entire>, and sums and compositions of <entire functions> are <entire>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\sinh z\text{ is holomorphic on all of }\mathbb C,\qquad (\sinh z)'=\cosh z.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The zeros of the <hyperbolic sine> are exactly $z=k\pi i$, $k\in\mathbb Z$, since $\sinh z=0$ is equivalent to $e^{2z}=1$. Each zero is simple because $\cosh(k\pi i)=(-1)^k\ne0$. The quotient is therefore <holomorphic> precisely on
$$
\boxed{\mathbb C\setminus\{k\pi i:k\in\mathbb Z\}.}
$$
At zero, the <Taylor expansion> $\sinh z=z+O(z^3)$ gives $z/\sinh^2z=1/z+O(z)$, so zero is a <simple pole>. At $k\pi i\ne0$, the denominator has a double zero and the numerator is nonzero, giving a <pole> of order two. Thus none of the omitted points admits a <holomorphic extension>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

The denominator vanishes exactly when $e^z=-1$, namely at $z=(2k+1)\pi i$, $k\in\mathbb Z$. Its <derivative> there is $e^z=-1\ne0$, so each such zero is simple and the numerator does not cancel it. Hence
$$
\boxed{\frac1{1+e^z}\text{ is holomorphic on }\mathbb C\setminus\{(2k+1)\pi i:k\in\mathbb Z\},}
$$
and every omitted point is a <simple pole>.

